/* POV passage, her side of the task Extra Friendly (task_sister_be_nice_to_brian), stage 0: the first time she keeps the
   hardware guy company because I asked her to. Opened with the CCTV arrow while the task runs, or from her end-of-day
   report. Effects are in the task's onComplete.
   Proposal 6.5b: a comedy with a small sting. Her first order about another man. Coffee, a lot of silence that she fills
   herself (he goes quiet around her), and the soldering iron collection her report line is about. Under the jokes: he
   asked her to be nice to another guy, and she does it because he asked.
   Clips (images/characters/sister/tasks/, activity extra_friendly0): she brings the coffee; she listens with her chin in
   her hand; the soldering irons */
<<silent>><<SetBackground $locations[13].img>><</silent>>
<<PovHeader $sister>>
Be nice to $brian.name. Fine. I'm nice to everybody, it's one of my better qualities, and the guy at the bagel place gets a smiley face on his receipt every single morning whether he deserves it or not.

Nobody's ever had to ask me before, though.

$player.name said it the way you'd ask someone to water the plants: be extra friendly to $brian.name for a while, he needs a little attention, and then straight back to his screen. I said sure, of course. What was I supposed to say, no thanks, I'd rather you wanted my attention yourself?

So, coffee. I asked $mom.callName how $brian.name takes it, because of course she knows, she knows everybody's coffee, which is a little terrifying. Two sugars. I carry it over, pull the spare chair up to his desk and sit down as if I do this every day.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $sister, text: "Hi. I brought you a coffee, two sugars. I asked around." },
{ who: $brian, text: "Oh. Um. Thanks." },
{ text: "He takes it without looking at me and sets it down on the far side of his keyboard, very carefully, as if it might go off." },
{ who: $sister, text: "What are you working on? It looks complicated." },
{ who: $brian, text: "Hm. Sensor board." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

That's the whole answer. I wait for more, and there isn't any more. He just looks at the board, and the tips of his ears go pink.

So I do the talking, which is fine, I'm good at that part. I tell him about my statistics professor, and the vending machine on the second floor that eats dollar bills, and the time it gave me two bags of pretzels for the price of none. He nods at the board and says "hm" in the right places. At the pretzels he actually laughs, a short, surprised sound, and then looks embarrassed about it.

I put my chin in my hand, and somewhere around there I notice I've stopped performing. It's kind of nice, sitting here. He doesn't need me to be anything.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $brian, text: "Do you, ehh... want to see something?" },
{ who: $sister, text: "Always." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

He opens his bottom drawer, and it's soldering irons. Seven of them, lined up on a folded towel, each with a little label in his handwriting. One of them says <i>do not lend</i>, so of course I ask.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $brian, text: "My cousin borrowed it. Once. There was a model train, and then, um. The fire department." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

That's all I get, and it's somehow better than any story he could have told. I laugh so hard I snort, and he almost looks at me.

I think where $brian.name is from, that drawer was a proposal.

When I get up, he says thank you for the coffee three times, once while I'm still standing there, once when I'm a few steps away and once to my empty chair.

On the way back to my desk I look over at $player.name, just to see if he saw any of it. He's on the phone. Maybe he saw, maybe he didn't, and I hate how much I want to know.

<<EndPov>>
